<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Al-Nasir Hasan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1334–<a href="/years/1361">1361</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as al-Nasir Badr ad-Din Hasan, Qamari, Sultan Hasan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Al-Nasir Hasan was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> sultan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-nasir-muhammad">al-Nasir Muhammad</a>, who reigned twice, in 1347–1351 and 1354–1361. Installed at the age of 12, he was at first a ceremonial ruler under the senior <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a> and was deposed in 1351 when he tried to assert his authority. Restored by a coup of the emirs Shaykhu and Sirghitmish, he purged the leading emirs through imprisonment, exile and execution and gave high ranks to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/awlad-al-nas">awlad al-nas</a>, the descendants of mamluks, on a scale unprecedented in the sultanate. He was killed in 1361 by one of his own mamluks, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yalbugha-al-umari">Yalbugha al-Umari</a>; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> Hasan Mosque-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">Madrasa</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>, begun in 1357, was completed in 1363.</p></div>
